<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Vestfold</h1><p class="st-meta">750 CE – 880 CE (approx.) · capital Skiringssal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Viking Age kingdom on the west shore of the Oslofjord, home of the Oseberg and Gokstad ship burials and of the dynasty that unified Norway.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Vestfold's rich farmland and harbours made it a centre of power by the 8th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borre-mound-cemetery">Borre</a> mounds hold one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia's</a> largest royal cemeteries, and Frankish annals of 813 record Danish kings campaigning in Vestfold to assert their overlordship, showing it lay in the Danish sphere.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kings controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaupang">Kaupang</a>, the trading town the traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohthere-of-halogaland">Ohthere</a> called Sciringes heal, where goods from the Frankish, Danish and Arab worlds were exchanged. Royal display reached its height in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship">Oseberg ship</a> burial of 834 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokstad-ship">Gokstad</a> burial around 900. Snorri's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ynglinga-saga">Ynglinga saga</a> traces the kings back to the Swedish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ynglings">Ynglings</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>According to the sagas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halfdan-the-black">Halfdan the Black</a> extended Vestfold's power over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upplands-norway">Upplands</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a> conquered western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, traditionally at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hafrsfjord">Hafrsfjord</a> around 872. Vestfold thus became the cradle of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway</a>, and its ship burials are among the best-known finds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/vestfold.jpg" alt="Vestfold" width="320" height="152"><figcaption>Image: Opphopp! · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F%C3%A6rder.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>750 CE – 880 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Skiringssal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Halfdan the Black, Harald Fairhair</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Vestfold</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=815&amp;focus=vestfold" aria-label="Vestfold on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4895 184 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Vestfold lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5215 440L5225 425"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5225 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5215 440h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5215 440h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=815&amp;focus=vestfold">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
